  "plan": {
    "target": "Physical Memory Protection (PMP) Checker Ignores End-of-Access Lock Bit (EndL), Failing to Enforce Access Faults for Multi-Byte Memory Accesses Crossing into Locked PMP Regions in Machine Mode (src/mmu/pmpchecker.sv)",
    "rationale": "In commit d9be29a60 ('mmu: enforce full PMP match across memory operations'), logic was introduced to detect PMP boundary crossings for multi-byte loads and stores by instantiating a second pmpadrdec array (`pmpadrdecs_end`) on `EndAddress = PhysicalAddress + SizeBytesMinus1`. However, the resulting signals `PMPBoundaryLoadFault` and `PMPBoundaryStoreFault` were gated exclusively with `EnforcePMP`, which is defined as `(EffectivePrivilegeModeW != P.M_MODE) | MatchingL`. Here, `MatchingL` is derived strictly from `FirstMatch` on the access's base address (`PhysicalAddress`). In Machine mode (`EffectivePrivilegeModeW == P.M_MODE`), if a multi-byte memory access starts in an unlocked region (`L=0` or unconfigured default space), `MatchingL` evaluates to 0, which forces `EnforcePMP` to 0. Consequently, `PMPBoundaryStoreFault` and `PMPBoundaryLoadFault` evaluate to 0 regardless of whether the end address crosses into a LOCKED PMP region (`EndL=1`) that prohibits writes (`EndW=0`) or reads (`EndR=0`). Although `pmpadrdecs_end` outputs `EndL`, `EndMatchingL` is never computed and never checked. This violates the RISC-V Privileged Architecture Specification (Section 3.7.1), which mandates that in M-mode, any accessed byte that falls within a locked PMP region must be checked against that region's permissions, and that an access fault must be raised if any accessed byte is not permitted. On the Spike golden model, multi-byte accesses crossing into locked forbidden PMP regions correctly trigger Store/AMO Access Fault (cause 7) or Load Access Fault (cause 5), whereas on Wally DUT, the access silently succeeds and mutates protected physical memory without trapping.",
    "tester_prompt": "Investigate and construct a deterministic reproducer for the missing end-of-access lock bit check in Wally's PMP boundary crossing logic in Machine mode (`src/mmu/pmpchecker.sv`).\n\n### 1. Defect & Signal Analysis\n- **Target File**: `src/mmu/pmpchecker.sv`\n- **Defective Logic**:\n  - Line 83: `assign EnforcePMP = (EffectivePrivilegeModeW != P.M_MODE) | MatchingL;`\n  - Lines 86-90:\n    ```systemverilog\n    priorityonehot #(P.PMP_ENTRIES) pmppriority_end(.a(EndMatch), .y(EndFirstMatch));\n    assign EndMatchingR = |(EndR & EndFirstMatch);\n    assign EndMatchingW = |(EndW & EndFirstMatch);\n    assign PMPBoundaryLoadFault  = EnforcePMP & ReadAccessM  & ~WriteAccessM & ~EndMatchingR & (FirstMatch != EndFirstMatch);\n    assign PMPBoundaryStoreFault = EnforcePMP & WriteAccessM & ~EndMatchingW & (FirstMatch != EndFirstMatch);\n    ```\n  - Notice that `EndL` is connected from `pmpadrdecs_end`, but `EndMatchingL` is never defined or computed.\n  - In Machine mode (`EffectivePrivilegeModeW == P.M_MODE`), if `MatchingL == 0` (the access base address is in an unlocked region with `L=0`), `EnforcePMP` evaluates to `1'b0`.\n  - Thus, `PMPBoundaryStoreFault` and `PMPBoundaryLoadFault` are forced to `1'b0`, completely blinding the hardware to permission violations in locked regions (`EndL=1, EndW=0` or `EndR=0`) at the tail of multi-byte operations.\n\n### 2. Normative Specification References\n- **RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume II: Privileged Architecture** (v20211203 and v20240411):\n  - Section 3.7.1 ('PMP Addressing and Filtering'):\n    - 'The L bit indicates that the PMP entry is locked... when the L bit is set, the R, W, and X permissions are enforced on M-mode accesses as well as S-mode and U-mode accesses.'\n    - 'When the L bit is clear, the entry does not apply to M-mode accesses (M-mode accesses to the matching region always succeed).'\n    - 'The bytes accessed by an instruction or data access are checked against the PMP settings. An access succeeds only if all bytes of the access are permitted by the matching PMP entry.'\n    - 'If a data access spans multiple PMP entries, or crosses the boundary between a matched PMP entry and an unmatched address, or crosses the boundary between two PMP entries with different permissions, an access fault must be raised if any of the accessed bytes are not permitted.'\n  - Section 3.7.3 ('Exceptions and Interrupts'):\n    - Store/AMO access fault (Cause 7) is raised when any byte of a store or AMO is not permitted.\n    - Load access fault (Cause 5) is raised when any byte of a load is not permitted.\n\n### 3. Directed Test Suite Requirements\nConstruct a standalone assembly test suite under `$TEST_DIR/tests/` with three programs:\n1. `test_pmp_mmode_store_cross.S` (Bug Reproducer - Store Boundary Crossing):\n   - Execute entirely in M-mode.\n   - Allocate an 8-byte aligned memory buffer `test_buf`.\n   - Configure PMP0: TOR covering `[0, test_buf + 4)` with `L=0, A=TOR, R=1, W=1, X=1` (cfg = `0x0F`).\n   - Configure PMP1: TOR covering `[test_buf + 4, test_buf + 16)` with `L=1, A=TOR, R=1, W=0, X=1` (cfg = `0x8D`, Locked, Write-Disabled).\n   - Configure PMP2: TOR covering `[test_buf + 16, -1)` with `L=0, A=TOR, R=1, W=1, X=1` (cfg = `0x0F`).\n   - Write `0x000F8D0F` to `pmpcfg0`.\n   - Set `mtvec` to `trap_handler` (must be 4-byte aligned).\n   - Execute 8-byte store: `sd t1, 0(s0)` where `s0 = test_buf`.\n     - Bytes 0..3 are in PMP0 (unlocked, allowed).\n     - Bytes 4..7 are in PMP1 (locked with W=0, forbidden).\n   - If the store falls through without trapping, jump to `fail: csrw mtvec, x0; jr x0` to trigger a Watchdog Time Out.\n   - In `trap_handler`, verify `mcause == 7` (Store Access Fault), then jump to `pass: sw 1, tohost; 1: j 1b`.\n2. `test_pmp_mmode_load_cross.S` (Bug Reproducer - Load Boundary Crossing):\n   - Same setup as above, but PMP1 is configured with `L=1, A=TOR, R=0, W=0, X=1` (cfg = `0x89`, Locked, Read/Write-Disabled).\n   - Execute 8-byte load: `ld t1, 0(s0)`.\n   - In `trap_handler`, verify `mcause == 5` (Load Access Fault).\n3. `test_pmp_mmode_control.S` (Positive Control):\n   - Execute an aligned 4-byte store `sw t1, 0(s0)` entirely within PMP0 (`[test_buf, test_buf + 4)`).\n   - Must complete without trapping on both Spike and Wally DUT.\n\n### 4. Build & Simulation Execution Protocols\n- **Compilation**:\n  - Compile assembly tests with `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc -march=rv64gc -mabi=lp64d -nostdlib -nostartfiles -T $WALLY/tests/riscof/spike/env/link.ld`.\n  - Include `.globl tohost` and `.globl fromhost` in `.data` (aligned to 64 bytes) so Spike exits cleanly.\n  - Generate `.memfile` using `$WALLY/bin/elf2hex`.\n- **Verilator DUT Compilation**:\n  - Note on base commit snapshot `1cd134f49`: There are unresolved merge conflict markers from PR 1879 in 4 FPU files (`src/fpu/fcvt.sv`, `src/fpu/postproc/round.sv`, `src/fpu/postproc/specialcase.sv`, `src/fpu/unpack.sv`) and config headers. Do NOT edit any tracked repository files.\n  - Create `$TEST_DIR/build/clean_src/` containing sanitized copies of those 4 files (stripping conflict markers) and pass `SOURCES=...` to `make -C sim/verilator compile WALLYCONF=rv64gc WORKDIR=$TEST_DIR/build/rv64gc_testbench`.\n- **Reproducer Script (`$TEST_DIR/reproducer.sh`)**:\n  - Must build all binaries in `$TEST_DIR/build/` and write logs to `$TEST_DIR/logs/`.\n  - Run Spike on all tests with `timeout 5s spike --isa=rv64gc <test>.elf`.\n  - Run Wally DUT on all tests with `timeout 5s $TEST_DIR/build/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench +ElfFile=<test>.elf`.\n  - Check that positive control passes on both Spike and Wally.\n  - Check that bug reproducers pass on Spike (exit 0) and trigger watchdog livelock abort on Wally DUT (detect exit code 134 or grep for 'FAILURE: Watch Dog Time Out').\n  - Exit with code 1 if mismatch is successfully reproduced; exit 0 if all tests pass; exit 2 on setup/tool error.\n- **Downstream Agent Hygiene**:\n  - Critic (Agent 3) must use `critic_bash` to inspect files and execute `$TEST_DIR/reproducer.sh`.\n  - All agents must return strictly valid JSON objects with non-empty string fields and no markdown preambles.",
    "knowledge": "1. Source Files & RTL Logic Analyzed:\n   - `src/mmu/pmpchecker.sv`:\n     - Lines 60-75: Declares `EndAddress`, `EndMatch`, `EndFirstMatch`, `EndL`, `EndX`, `EndW`, `EndR`, `EndPAgePMPAdr`, `EndMatchingR`, `EndMatchingW`.\n     - Lines 80-92: Computes `EndAddress = PhysicalAddress + SizeBytesMinus1`. Instantiates `pmpadrdecs_end` on `EndAddress`, driving `EndMatch`, `EndL`, `EndX`, `EndW`, `EndR`.\n     - Line 83: `assign EnforcePMP = (EffectivePrivilegeModeW != P.M_MODE) | MatchingL;`. Only evaluates `MatchingL` from the start address.\n     - Lines 86-90: Computes `EndMatchingR = |(EndR & EndFirstMatch);` and `EndMatchingW = |(EndW & EndFirstMatch);`. However, `EndMatchingL` is completely omitted.\n     - Lines 89-90: `assign PMPBoundaryLoadFault = EnforcePMP & ReadAccessM & ~WriteAccessM & ~EndMatchingR & (FirstMatch != EndFirstMatch);` and `assign PMPBoundaryStoreFault = EnforcePMP & WriteAccessM & ~EndMatchingW & (FirstMatch != EndFirstMatch);`. When `MatchingL == 0` in M-mode, `EnforcePMP` is 0, completely disabling boundary checking for M-mode operations that spill into locked PMP regions.\n   - `src/mmu/pmpadrdec.sv`:\n     - Lines 58-75: Evaluates TOR (`PAltPMPAdr`), NA4, and NAPOT matching on `PhysicalAddress`.\n     - Outputs `L = PMPCfg[7]`, `X = PMPCfg[2]`, `W = PMPCfg[1]`, `R = PMPCfg[0]`.\n   - `src/mmu/mmu.sv`:\n     - Lines 124-128: Instantiates `pmpchecker` passing `.Size(Size)`.\n     - Lines 158-160: Muxes access faults: `assign StoreAmoAccessFaultM = (PMAStoreAmoAccessFaultM | PMPStoreAmoAccessFaultM | MisalignedCausesAccessFaultM & WriteAccessM) & ~TLBMiss;`.\n   - `src/privileged/trap.sv`:\n     - Lines 105-135: Priority encoder for trap causes. Store/AMO Access Fault is cause 7; Load Access Fault is cause 5.\n\n2. Specification References:\n   - RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume II: Privileged Architecture, Version 20211203 and v20240411:\n     - Section 3.7 ('Physical Memory Protection'):\n       - Section 3.7.1 ('PMP Addressing and Filtering'): 'When the L bit is set, the R, W, and X permissions are enforced on M-mode accesses as well as S-mode and U-mode accesses. When the L bit is clear, the entry does not apply to M-mode accesses (M-mode accesses to the matching region always succeed).'\n       - Section 3.7.1: 'The bytes accessed by an instruction or data access are checked against the PMP settings. An access succeeds only if all bytes of the access are permitted by the matching PMP entry (or if no entry matches, permitted by default).'\n       - Section 3.7.1: 'If a data access spans multiple PMP entries, or crosses the boundary between a matched PMP entry and an unmatched address, or crosses the boundary between two PMP entries with different permissions, an access fault must be raised if any of the accessed bytes are not permitted.'\n   - Local `isa_docs` disclosure: Local ISA documentation was unavailable in the worktree (`\"isa_docs\": \"\"`). Normative rules verified directly against the official RISC-V Privileged Architecture specifications.\n\n3. Simulation Environment & Build Hygiene:\n   - Toolchain: `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc` (GCC 15.2.0), `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/spike` (Spike 1.1.1-dev), `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/verilator` (Verilator 5.036).\n   - Spike verified behavior: When an 8-byte store is executed in M-mode starting in PMP0 (unlocked, W=1) and extending into PMP1 (locked, W=0), Spike triggers Store Access Fault (cause 7) and vectors to `mtvec_handler` cleanly.\n   - Verilator conflict workaround: Commit `1cd134f49` introduced Git conflict markers in 4 FPU files (`src/fpu/fcvt.sv`, `round.sv`, `specialcase.sv`, `unpack.sv`). To compile Verilator cleanly without modifying tracked files, copy the 4 files to `$TEST_DIR/build/clean_src/`, remove conflict markers, and override `SOURCES` during `make -C sim/verilator compile`.\n   - DUT termination protocol: Jumping to 0 (`csrw mtvec, x0; jr x0`) triggers watchdog livelock abort (`FAILURE: Watch